WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.010
Film Courage:&nbsp; Can you help us determine
a Stage versus a Turning Point [regarding

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.010
the six-state plot structure for screenwriting]?

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.010
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; Yes.&nbsp;

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.370
Basically a Stage is a sequence of events
in the story.&nbsp;

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.849
It covers a span of time.&nbsp;

00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:19.330
A Turning Point is a single event that happens
to the hero.&nbsp;

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:24.689
So a stage would be, we meet the hero, the
hero is living their everyday life.&nbsp;

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:27.949
We maybe meet the villain in the piece.&nbsp;

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:34.690
We meet other characters, the best friend,
something like that or in stage two she arrives

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:40.180
at this new job, if that is her new situation,
we meet the boss, she has some problems, she

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:45.320
has co-workers, she meets the guy who is going
to be the romance character or the love interest.&nbsp;

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.270
Those are the stages, they are sequences of
events but the opportunity is going to be

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:55.460
a single event that happens to her such as
(in that example I just made up) the opportunity

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.550
would be she gets the new job.&nbsp;

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.980
Ok?&nbsp;

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:09.090
Or the change of plans would be she is told
she can have a promotion if she can design

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:14.570
a new house for a billionaire and so her goal
in the movie becomes to design a house for

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.040
a billionaire.&nbsp;

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.500
By the way, I’m making these examples up
that’s why that would be a terrible movie…so

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:26.579
anyone listening to this don’t take that
as an example of a good idea you should pursue.&nbsp;

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:30.640
I shouldn’t make stuff up off the top of
my head.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:37.649
Let’s take an example from a real movie,
one of my favorites this year, Jason Bourne.&nbsp;

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:38.799
O.k.

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:40.510
stage one we see him.&nbsp;

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.500
He’s down in Turkey I think?&nbsp;

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.250
Or the border of Turkey?&nbsp;

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.460
He earns money by getting into fights and
knocking these British guys out.&nbsp;

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:56.880
He’s obviously unhappy and we see that he’s
just sort of stuck in this identity of a guy

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.049
who has no home, no connection and no real
identity in the traditional sense.&nbsp;

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:03.221
He doesn’t really know who he is.&nbsp;

00:02:03.221 --> 00:02:07.430
Is he Jason Borne or is he really the guy
who became Jason Bourne and so on?&nbsp;

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:08.920
So all of that is a sequence.&nbsp;

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:14.670
It’s a whole series of events that’s a
stage and we cut away from him during that

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:21.650
stage and see the Julia Stiles character and
she breaks in to this facility that&nbsp; the

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:25.530
CIA runs or the NSA or whatever it is and
she steals these things.&nbsp;

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:26.550
We don’t know why.&nbsp;

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:27.550
That’s a stage.&nbsp;

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.550
It’s a whole lot of scenes strung together.&nbsp;

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.870
The opportunity is a single event.&nbsp;

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:38.260
He’s in this fight and he sees she shows
up in his life and is coming to him and now

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.300
he’s in a new situation.&nbsp;

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:44.440
What am I going to do now that this woman
from my past who knows the truth of who I

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:49.210
am, who I apparently had an affair with once
before I lost my memory and so on?&nbsp;

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:50.210
What am I going to do?&nbsp;

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:53.500
And so now it’s a new sequence of events.&nbsp;

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.970
She tells him what’s going on they have
to go on the run from a guy.&nbsp;

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:02.420
She has to give him the flashlight, etc. and
we see new villains and we see what’s going

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:06.450
on a the NSA as they try and stop him.&nbsp;

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:10.500
So again, a Stage is a sequence of events.&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.100
A Turning Point is a single event that happens
to the hero.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:14.120
QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS:&nbsp; What is the first
turning point in the last movie you saw?

